{
  "element_layout": {
    "score": 14,
    "feedback": "The six sampled frames use readable light text on a dark background, a stable title, and clearly separated rows. Cyan, purple, and orange reliably connect each class with its values and bars. However, the graphics occupy relatively little of the available space, leaving large empty areas while explanatory text remains modest in size. At 4.00 seconds, the exponential bars are extremely small and hard to interpret. The shared-total frame at 8.00 seconds shows only a number, missing an opportunity to arrange all three contributions visibly around their sum. The final stacked bar is useful but spatially separated from its class labels. These samples do not establish a persistent presentation-left/animation-right division; the assessment therefore concerns the layouts actually visible."
  },
  "attractiveness": {
    "score": 12,
    "feedback": "The restrained dark theme and three contrasting class colors create a clean, coherent appearance. The final segmented probability bar provides a meaningful visual payoff. Nevertheless, the sampled presentation is dominated by text and plain rectangles, with limited visual variety or emphasis. The large unused regions and repeated header make several stages feel visually sparse. The shared-total idea could become more engaging through a visible collection of the three colored masses into one whole. Animation fluidity, effects, and sustained engagement cannot be judged from still frames."
  },
  "logic_flow": {
    "score": 15,
    "feedback": "The sampled sequence follows an appropriate scaffold: signed inputs at 1.00 seconds, exponential values at 4.00 and 6.50 seconds, a shared sum at 8.00 seconds, division by that sum at 11.00 seconds, and normalized probabilities at 17.50 seconds. This progression aligns directly with the learning objective. However, the intermediate total and division stages remove the visible bars, so the learner must mentally connect the original masses to the final segments. The repeated sentence also mentions a total of 1 before clearly distinguishing it from the unnormalized total of 6.051. Actual pacing, transitions, narration, and any explanations between the supplied timestamps are unavailable for evaluation."
  },
  "accuracy_depth": {
    "score": 16,
    "feedback": "The displayed mathematics is correct to the stated precision: exp(-1) is approximately 0.368, exp(0.7) approximately 2.014, and exp(1.3) approximately 3.669; their unrounded sum rounds to 6.051. Normalizing yields approximately 0.061, 0.333, and 0.606, whose displayed values sum to 1. The 6.50-second bars and final stacked segments appear consistent with the corresponding ratios. The example appropriately includes a negative input that becomes a positive mass. However, the visible text does not explicitly state that exponentiation makes every real input positive, nor provide the general softmax formula. Rounded calculations use equality signs rather than approximation signs. The statement that the three terms ultimately form total 1 needs clearer qualification: normalized probabilities sum to 1, whereas the exponential masses sum to approximately 6.051. These are mathematical observations separate from aesthetic judgments; no unseen content is assumed."
  },
  "visual_consistency": {
    "score": 17,
    "feedback": "Across the samples, background, typography, title placement, and class colors remain consistent. The same colors connect input labels, exponential masses, division expressions, and final probabilities, supporting visual tracking. Formatting is generally uniform. English stage labels embedded in an otherwise Chinese presentation slightly weaken language cohesion. The disappearance of graphics during intermediate stages and relocation into a final stacked bar reduce spatial continuity, although the unseen transitions may provide connections. Consistency of motion, timing, and interpolation cannot be assessed from the supplied stills."
  },
  "overall_score": 74,
  "summary": "Based only on six timestamped still frames, this is a mathematically sound and logically ordered introduction to softmax. It shows the intended numerical transformation and ends with a useful representation of probabilities sharing total 1. Its teaching would improve by explicitly explaining positivity and preserving visual links from separate exponential masses through their shared denominator to normalized segments. These scores are provisional assessments of sampled visual evidence, not verification of the complete video, audio, pacing, or animation quality.",
  "strengths": [
    "Correct numerical example containing both negative and positive inputs.",
    "Clear progression from logits through exponentiation and common-total division to probabilities.",
    "Stable class colors support tracking across the sampled stages.",
    "Final stacked bar communicates a shared probability total of 1."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Explicitly state that exp(z) is positive for every real input, highlighting the transformation of -1 into approximately 0.368.",
    "Display p_i = exp(z_i) / sum_j exp(z_j) alongside the worked example and use approximation signs for rounded values.",
    "Visually assemble the three colored masses into their shared total of approximately 6.051 before showing normalization.",
    "Keep the masses visible during division and connect them clearly to the final probability segments.",
    "Distinguish the exponential-mass total from the normalized probability total in the persistent explanatory text.",
    "Enlarge the central graphics, place labels nearer their corresponding marks, and use consistent instructional language."
  ]
}
